UseIPSec [SimTalk]
Sets if the Socket Interface designated by <Path> uses the IPSec network protocol suite for the Socket
communication (true) or not (false).
Remarks
Plant Simulation uses the default settings of IPSec. IPSec stands for Internet Protocol Security.
Note
You have to configure IPSec at operation system level beforehand to ensure that encryption works
correctly.
Type
Attribute
Syntax
<Path>.UseIPSec:boolean
Assignment Value
You can assign a value of data type boolean.
Example
Socket.UseIPSec := true
See also
Use IPSec [check box]
UseIPSec [SimTalk]
14-430 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

UseRFC1006 [SimTalk]
Sets if the Socket Interface designated by <Path> adds an RFC 1006 protocol header to all sent data, which
contains the length of the data package (true) or not (false).
Remarks
This ensures that the receiver receives the data packages with the size in which they were sent.
RFC-1006 with the title ISO Transport Service on top of the TCP is a protocol extension of the TCP protocol.
In addition to the TCP data, it transmits further information to provide certain services for the user.
Note
This setting has to be enabled on both sides, sender and receiver, and before the connection is made to
work properly.
Type
Attribute
Syntax
<Path>.UseRFC1006:boolean
Assignment Value
You can assign a value of data type boolean.
Example
Socket.UseRFC1006 := true
See also
Use RFC 1006 [check box]
MQTT
MQTT
Use the object MQTT for transmitting messages from device to device over the Internet using an open
network protocol. MQTT stands for Message Queuing Telemetry Transport.
UseRFC1006 [SimTalk]
Plant Simulation Help 14-431
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Image
Description
According to https://mqtt.org “MQTT is an OASIS standard messaging protocol for the Internet of Things
(IoT). It is designed as an extremely lightweight publish/subscribe messaging transport that is ideal for
connecting remote devices with a small code footprint and minimal network bandwidth. MQTT today is
used in a wide variety of industries, such as automotive, manufacturing, telecommunications, oil and gas,
etc.”
The MQTT Interface provides a connection between Plant Simulation and an MQTT message broker. Plant
Simulation can send and receive MQTT messages. You can, for example, use the MQTT Interface to
connect AGVs with a control system or a fleet manager.
As the quality of service, that ensures the reliability of message delivery to the subscriber, 0 is preset. The
MQTT Interface attempts to deliver MQTT messages only once. The Client does not have to confirm that it
received the transmitted message, and it is not ensured that the message arrives in any case.
Consult the file ReadMe_OSS.html in the Plant Simulation installation folder for information about the
open-source software we use. The default installation folder for Plant Simulation 2606 is C:\Program
Files\Siemens\Tecnomatix Plant Simulation 2606.
The Profiler shows the entire MQTT Communication Statistics.
To show a tooltip with information about the MQTT Interface, hover with the mouse over it.
To change the length of the graphic and the anchor points of the MQTT Interface, click Show
Manipulators 
  on the Edit ribbon tab or press M on the keyboard.
Add the Object to the Simulation Model
To add the object MQTT to the Class Library, click 
  Manage Class Library > Basic Objects >
Information Flow > MQTT on the Home ribbon tab.
